-- title:
--   Cubes of side $h$ over points of an $h\sqrt{d}$-shrunk set stay in the half-shrunk set
-- statement:
--   Work in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$. For $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ and a radius $r$ let
--   $$S_r \;=\; \{\, y \in S \;:\; \overline{B}(y, r) \subseteq S \,\}$$
--   be the shrinking of $S$ by $r$, and let $S[x] = x + S$ be the translate of $S$ by $x$.
--
--   Let $h \in \mathbb{R}$ and $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^d$, and suppose that
--   $$y \in x + S_{h\sqrt{d}}.$$
--   Then the open cube of side $h$ centred at $y$ satisfies
--   $$A_h(y) \;\subseteq\; x + S_{\frac{h}{2}\sqrt{d}}.$$
--
--   This is the scale-$h$ form of the statement that a tile is contained in a slightly less shrunk copy of a set as soon as its centre lies in the more shrunk copy; the loss $h\sqrt{d} \to \tfrac{h}{2}\sqrt{d}$ is exactly the circumradius of a cube of side $h$. Because every constant scales linearly in $h$, the lemma is the form in which the geometry survives the passage from mesh $1$ to mesh $h$, which is the rescaling used to pass between the lattice $\mathbb{Z}^d$ and the lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No positivity hypothesis is placed on $h$; for $h \le 0$ the cube $A_h(y)$ is empty and the inclusion holds vacuously.
